## Proof

Let $g$ be the genus of $S$. We show that such a meromorphic function always exists, using the Riemann–Roch theorem.

**Setup.** For a positive integer $n$, consider the divisor $D_n = n\cdot q$ on $S$. The Riemann–Roch space
$$L(D_n) = \{f \text{ meromorphic on } S : \operatorname{ord}_x(f) \geq -D_n(x)\ \forall x\}$$
consists of all meromorphic functions on $S$ whose only possible pole is at $q$, of order at most $n$. By the Riemann–Roch theorem, for $n \geq 2g-1$:
$$\dim L(D_n) = \deg D_n + 1 - g = n + 1 - g.$$

**Key observation.** Since $p \neq q$, every $f \in L(D_n)$ is holomorphic at $p$. Hence the evaluation map
$$\operatorname{ev}_p : L(D_n) \to \mathbb{C}, \quad f \mapsto f(p)$$
is a well-defined linear functional. It is nonzero, because the constant function $\mathbf{1} \in L(D_n)$ satisfies $\operatorname{ev}_p(\mathbf{1}) = 1$. Therefore the subspace
$$V := \ker(\operatorname{ev}_p) = \{f \in L(D_n) : f(p) = 0\}$$
has dimension
$$\dim V = \dim L(D_n) - 1 = n - g.$$

**Choosing $n$.** Take $n = 2g+1$. Then $n \geq 2g-1$ (so the Riemann–Roch formula applies) and $n \geq g+1$ (so $\dim V \geq 1$). Concretely,
$$\dim V = (2g+1) - g = g + 1 \geq 1.$$
Thus $V$ contains a nonzero function $f$.

**Verification.** We claim this $f$ satisfies $f(p)=0$ and $f(q)\neq 0$.

- **$f(p) = 0$:** Immediate from $f \in V = \ker(\operatorname{ev}_p)$.

- **$f(q) \neq 0$:** The function $f$ is a nonzero meromorphic function on the compact Riemann surface $S$. If $f$ had no poles at all, it would be holomorphic on all of $S$, hence constant (by the maximum principle on a compact surface). Then $f(p)=0$ would force $f \equiv 0$, contradicting the choice of $f \neq 0$. So $f$ must have at least one pole. Since $f \in L(D_n)$, the only point where $f$ can have a pole is $q$. Therefore $f$ has a pole at $q$, which means $q$ is **not** a zero of $f$; equivalently $f(q) = \infty \neq 0$.

**Conclusion.** The function $f$ is meromorphic on $S$, vanishes at $p$, and does not vanish at $q$. Hence such a function always exists for any compact Riemann surface $S$ and any two distinct points $p, q \in S$.

